    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **34 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the string `membershipTier` with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133):\n\n**`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Swapped the `membershipTier` (string) response field for `membershipLevel` (`integer`, min 1, max 3), so the generated `OwnerDto` now exposes `membershipLevel` instead. The tier field is gone from the response.\n\n**`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Replaced the `membershipTier(Owner)` helper (with its GOLD/SILVER/BRONZE logic) with `membershipLevel(Owner)`: start at 1, `+1` when an email is present, `+1` when `namesakeCount == 0`, capped at 3. The `@Mapping` now targets `membershipLevel`. The old GOLD-by-household rule is dropped (level 4 is reserved for tenure), so a 3-member household no longer confers a special level.\n\n**`rest/function/owner/AuditOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 The create audit line now records `membershipLevel` alongside the owner id (it injects `OwnerMapper` so the level is computed from a single source of truth rather than duplicating the rule).\n\nI also cleaned up two now-stale comments that referenced the removed GOLD tier (in `AssignHouseholdMemberCount.java` and `owners.POST.yml`). The `householdMemberCount` field is still computed and persisted but no longer drives any level.\n\nThe observed audit output confirms the behaviour end-to-end, e.g. `membershipLevel=3` (email + namesake 0), `=2` (namesake 0, no email), `=1` (namesake \u22651, no email).",
